          <p>
            On paper the DELTA 3 Plus and the Elite 100 V2 are nearly the same machine: 1024 Wh, 1800W continuous, 4,000 cycles to 80 percent, 1000W of solar input and sub-10 ms UPS switchover. The differences are at the edges. The Bluetti is 2.6 pounds lighter, which makes it the lightest 1 kWh unit in this comparison and noticeably easier to carry to a campsite or up from a basement. The EcoFlow charges faster from the wall, reaching full in under an hour where the Bluetti quotes 80 percent in 45 minutes from a smaller 1200W charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            The deciding difference is expansion. The DELTA 3 Plus accepts EcoFlow's extra batteries and grows to 5 kWh, so it can start as a camping unit and turn into a serious outage system later. Bluetti does not list an expansion battery for the Elite 100 V2, so what you buy is what you have. If there is any chance you will want two or three days of fridge runtime later, that alone settles it in EcoFlow's favour; our guide to whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">portable power station can run a refrigerator</a> works through how fast a fridge drains 1 kWh.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AC180 is the value play. It holds 128 Wh more than either rival, has a 1440W charger that reaches 80 percent in 45 minutes, and on Bluetti's own store it has recently sold for well under the other two. The costs are weight, at 35.3 pounds, half the solar input at 500W, and a slower 20 ms UPS switchover. Bluetti says it works with its B80, B230 and B300 batteries, but those connect through the DC input in power bank mode, feeding the AC180 one way rather than expanding it the way an EcoFlow extra battery does. Treat it as a fixed-capacity unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power Lifting on both Bluettis and X-Boost on the EcoFlow do the same job: they let resistive loads above the inverter rating, such as a 2000W kettle or hair dryer, run by lowering the voltage. That is fine for heating elements and useless for anything with a motor or electronics, so do not count it as real output. On real output all three are tied at 1800W, enough for a full-size microwave or a 1500W space heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict at this size: the DELTA 3 Plus for home backup, because it can grow and charges fastest. The Elite 100 V2 if weight and solar input top your list and 1 kWh is all you will ever need. The AC180 if you want the most capacity for the least money and can live with the weight. If you see the older EcoFlow DELTA 2, the best overall pick in our main guide, at a good discount, it remains a capable expandable 1 kWh unit too.
          </p>
