          <p>
            The DELTA 3 Max Plus's 3000W inverter is the headline. That is enough to start most 120V well pumps, run a window air conditioner and a fridge together, or run a full-size microwave with other loads on, where 2400W and 2600W start to get tight. It is also the lightest of the three by a wide margin, 13 pounds lighter than the AC200L, and its sub-10 ms switchover is the fastest here. For a unit that will live in a utility room as outage backup, it is the strongest all-round package.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AC200L earns its place with solar and price. Its 1200W solar input accepts a much wider voltage window than the 60V ceiling on most portable units, which lets you string three or four panels in series and run a long, thin cable from a sunny corner of the yard without overloading the input; our guide on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">how to charge a portable power station with solar</a> explains why that matters on cold mornings. It also has a 30A RV outlet, and its 2400W charger is the fastest wall input in this group. Expansion runs to roughly 7.6 kWh with Bluetti's current B300K battery, or about 8.2 kWh with two of the older B300 packs if you can still find them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weaknesses are weight, at 61.6 pounds, and the slowest UPS switchover here at 20 ms, which most network gear and desktop computers ride through but some older power supplies do not. Price is the wild card. The AC200L's list price is in the same band as the EcoFlow, but it has been discounted heavily and often, at times to well under $1,000, and at those prices it is the best capacity per dollar in this comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Elite 200 V2 is the odd one out: fixed capacity, but with a 6,000-plus cycle rating and Bluetti's figure of a 17-year expected service life, which is the longest-lived battery at this size. It weighs 53.4 pounds and offers 2600W. Choose it if you want a 2 kWh unit you will never expand and plan to cycle hard, for example running it daily off solar at a cabin. If expansion is on the table at all, one of the other two is the better foundation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict at this size: the DELTA 3 Max Plus for most home backup buyers, on output, weight and switchover speed. The AC200L if you plan to lean on solar or can buy it at a deep discount. The Elite 200 V2 if you want the longest cycle life in a fixed box. For complete panel-and-station bundles at this size, our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generator</a> roundup compares the kits directly.
          </p>
