        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Charging, Solar, UPS, App and Warranty
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery chemistry and cycle ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Every current model in this comparison uses LiFePO4, lithium iron phosphate, cells. That trades a little energy density for far longer cycle life and better thermal stability than the NMC lithium-ion used in older power stations, and our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> covers why that is worth paying for. Because both brands rate to 80 percent, their cycle numbers line up directly. EcoFlow quotes 3,000 cycles on the River 3 models and 4,000 on the DELTA 3 and DELTA Pro 3. Bluetti ranges from 3,000 on the AC200L and Elite 30 V2 to 6,000 on the Elite 200 V2 and Apex 300.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice even 3,000 cycles is more than most owners will ever use. A unit that is fully cycled once a week, which is heavy use for backup or camping, takes nearly 60 years to reach 3,000 cycles. The higher Bluetti ratings matter if you plan daily solar cycling, at a cabin or as part of a load-shifting setup; for an outage box they are a tiebreaker, not a reason to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall charging and solar input</h3>

          <p>
            EcoFlow is faster from the wall at most sizes, with its X-Stream charging filling the DELTA 3 Plus in 56 minutes. Bluetti's TurboBoost is close behind at 1 kWh, at 80 percent in about 45 minutes, and the AC200L's 2400W input is actually the quickest at 2 kWh. For solar, the brands trade blows: EcoFlow leads at 1 kWh against the AC180 but ties the Elite 100 V2 at 1000W, the AC200L leads at 2 kWh on wattage and voltage window, and the DELTA Pro 3 leads at the top with its high-voltage input. Both accept third-party panels within their voltage limits; our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> guide covers matching panels to these inputs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UPS switchover</h3>

          <p>
            Both brands sell their units as uninterruptible power supplies. EcoFlow quotes under 10 ms across the DELTA 3 family and the River 3 Plus, and 10 ms on the DELTA Pro 3. Bluetti varies more by model: 10 ms on the Elite 30 V2 and Elite 100 V2, 15 ms on the Elite 200 V2, 20 ms on the AC180 and AC200L, and 0 ms in the Apex 300's online mode. If you are buying to keep a desktop PC or a home server alive through flickers, prefer anything at 10 ms or below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">App and warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Both apps connect over Wi-Fi and Bluetooth on most models and let you monitor input and output, toggle outlets and set charge limits; the AC180 is Bluetooth only. EcoFlow's app is the more ambitious, with time-of-use charging and a storm mode that tops the battery up when severe weather is forecast. On warranty, EcoFlow lists five years on its current DELTA models, described as three years standard plus two on app registration. Bluetti lists five years on the AC180, AC200L, Elite 30 V2, Elite 200 V2 and Apex 300. Buy from the maker or an authorised seller, register on day one and keep the receipt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy EcoFlow if your priority is a home backup system that can grow and recharge quickly. The DELTA 3 Plus is the 1 kWh unit to start with if you might expand later, because Bluetti's lighter Elite 100 V2 cannot. The DELTA 3 Max Plus is the 2 kWh unit to choose if you need 3000W for a well pump or a window air conditioner alongside a fridge. And EcoFlow's faster wall charging is what you want when a storm is forecast and the battery is half empty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Bluetti if you value cycle life, low weight at 1 kWh or the most capacity for the money. The Elite 100 V2 is the lightest 1 kWh unit here with a full 1000W of solar input. The AC180 and AC200L, when discounted, are the best capacity per dollar in their classes, and the AC200L's wide solar window makes it the better 2 kWh unit for a real solar setup. At the top end, the Apex 300 is the pick if you want a 0 ms UPS mode, a 6,000-cycle battery and a home system you can grow one module at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn, decide by the one number that matters most. Need to expand a 1 kWh unit later? EcoFlow. Need the lightest 1 kWh box? Bluetti. Need more than 2600W from a 2 kWh unit? EcoFlow. Need the widest solar voltage window at 2 kWh? Bluetti. Need the most capacity in a single whole-home box? EcoFlow. Need zero switchover? Bluetti. Most buyers find one of those questions settles it in seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            For small units the choice is mostly about weight and output. The River 3 is the lightest and cheapest way into LiFePO4 for device charging. The River 3 Plus and Elite 30 V2 both give you 600W; pick the EcoFlow if you might add a battery, the Bluetti if you want the lighter box. Either way, do not expect any unit under 300 Wh to back up a house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever brand you lean toward, check it against the Jackery and Anker options before you buy. All of them, with the specific use each suits best, are in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a>, and our <a href="/jackery-vs-ecoflow/">Jackery vs EcoFlow</a> head to head covers the other brand pairing most buyers ask about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting Power Lifting or X-Boost as real output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bluetti's Power Lifting and EcoFlow's X-Boost let resistive loads like kettles and hair dryers run above the inverter rating by lowering voltage. They do nothing for motors, compressors or electronics. Compare the continuous output figure, 1800W, 2400W, 3000W and so on, when deciding what a unit can actually run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Bluetti battery truly expands every Bluetti unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The AC180 can draw from B80, B230 and B300 batteries only through its DC input in power bank mode, which is one-way charging rather than true expansion, and the Elite 100 V2 lists no expansion battery at all. If you want to grow capacity later, check the specific model's expansion support before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 1 kWh when the plan is whole-day backup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1 kWh unit runs an efficient fridge for roughly a day at best, and less once you add lights, a router and phone charging. If you need to ride out a full day without sun or a generator, start at 2 kWh or buy a 1 kWh unit that genuinely expands, such as the DELTA 3 Plus.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring UPS switchover time for computers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20 ms switchover, as on the AC180 and AC200L, is fine for most routers and laptops but can reset some desktop PCs and older power supplies. If the unit will sit between the wall and a computer or server, choose a model rated at 10 ms or below, or the Apex 300 in online mode.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying list price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands run sales so often that list prices are close to fiction; the AC200L and DELTA 3 Max Plus have both been offered at less than half of list. Set a price alert, compare the maker's store with major retailers, and do not buy at full price unless you need the unit before the next sale.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Bluetti or EcoFlow better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither wins outright. EcoFlow is better for most home backup buyers because of its faster wall charging, its 3000W expandable 2 kWh unit and its more mature home ecosystem. Bluetti is better if you want higher cycle ratings, the lightest 1 kWh unit, a 0 ms UPS mode on the Apex 300 or the lowest price per watt-hour when its units are discounted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better, the Bluetti Elite 100 V2 or the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They share 1024 Wh, 1800W output, 4,000 cycles to 80 percent, 1000W of solar and sub-10 ms UPS. The Elite 100 V2 is 2.6 pounds lighter. The DELTA 3 Plus charges faster from the wall and expands to 5 kWh, which the Bluetti cannot. Choose the EcoFlow for backup that may grow, the Bluetti for portability.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bluetti AC200L better than the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max Plus?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The DELTA 3 Max Plus has more output at 3000W against 2400W, a faster 10 ms class switchover and 13 pounds less weight. The AC200L has more solar input with a wider voltage window, a faster 2400W charger and a 30A RV outlet, and it is often discounted more deeply. Most backup buyers should prefer the EcoFlow; solar-heavy and budget buyers the Bluetti.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Bluetti and EcoFlow both use LiFePO4 batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every current model compared here uses LiFePO4 cells, and both brands rate cycle life to 80 percent of original capacity, so their numbers compare directly. EcoFlow quotes 3,000 to 4,000 cycles on current models and Bluetti 3,000 to 6,000. If you buy older or used units, check the spec sheet, since some early models from both brands used NMC cells.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which charges faster, Bluetti or EcoFlow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EcoFlow at most sizes. The DELTA 3 Plus fills from empty in 56 minutes on a 1500W charger, while Bluetti's Elite 100 V2 and AC180 reach 80 percent in about 45 minutes. At 2 kWh the AC200L's 2400W input is the quickest on paper. On big home units, headline times assume more power than a standard 15A outlet supplies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Bluetti panels with EcoFlow or the other way round?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Both brands accept third-party solar panels within each unit's voltage and wattage window, sometimes with an adapter cable for the connector. Expansion batteries are a different matter: they are brand and model specific, so an EcoFlow extra battery will not work with a Bluetti station, and not every battery works with every unit in the same brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which brand has the better warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are effectively tied on paper at five years. EcoFlow describes its five years on current DELTA models as three standard plus two on app registration. Bluetti lists five years on its current AC, Elite and Apex models. In both cases, buy from the maker or an authorised retailer and register the unit promptly to secure the full term.
              </p>
            </div>

